/* Geometry daily lessons follow uploaded packet order. */
const GEOMETRY={"topics": [{"id": "geo-radicals", "title": "Simplifying Radicals", "desc": "A square root reverses squaring. √a means the principal, nonnegative square root. √25 = 5, while solving x² = 25 gives x = ±5.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 1 · Day 1", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 1, "parts": [["Square roots and number types", "A square root reverses squaring. √a means the principal, nonnegative square root. √25 = 5, while solving x² = 25 gives x = ±5."], ["Simplify using a perfect-square factor", "To simplify √72, find a perfect-square factor: √(36 × 2) = 6√2. Keep factoring until no perfect square greater than 1 remains inside."], ["Variable radicals and absolute value", "√(x²) = |x|, not always x. For example, if x = −3, the root is 3. If a problem states x ≥ 0, then √(x²) = x."]], "trap": "√(a + b) is not generally √a + √b. A radical symbol alone gives the nonnegative root.", "example": "√98 = √(49 × 2) = 7√2. The factor 49 is a perfect square.", "model": "geo-radical", "day": 1, "constructions": []}, {"id": "geo-radical-operations", "title": "Adding and Subtracting Radicals", "desc": "Combine coefficients only when the simplified radicands match. √8 + √18 = 2√2 + 3√2 = 5√2.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 1 · Day 2", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 1, "parts": [["Like radicals", "Combine coefficients only when the simplified radicands match. √8 + √18 = 2√2 + 3√2 = 5√2."], ["Simplify before combining", "Simplify every radical before combining terms. For √8 + √18, extract square factors to get 2√2 + 3√2 = 5√2."], ["Handle subtraction carefully", "Distribute a subtraction sign to every term it affects. 4√3 − (√3 + 2√5) = 4√3 − √3 − 2√5 = 3√3 − 2√5."]], "trap": "Adding roots is different from multiplying roots. √2 + √3 does not become √5.", "example": "√20 + √45 = 2√5 + 3√5 = 5√5. Simplify before combining coefficients.", "model": "geo-radical", "day": 2, "constructions": []}, {"id": "geo-radical-products", "title": "Multiplying and Dividing Radicals", "desc": "Multiply coefficients and radicands, then simplify. Use distribution: √2(3 + √2) = 3√2 + 2. For two binomials, multiply all four pairs.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 1 · Day 3", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 1, "parts": [["Multiply every needed pair", "Multiply coefficients and radicands, then simplify. Use distribution: √2(3 + √2) = 3√2 + 2. For two binomials, multiply all four pairs."], ["Divide and simplify", "For a ≥ 0 and b > 0, √a/√b = √(a/b). Simplify the fraction, then the radical. For example, √48/√3 = 4."], ["Multiply two binomials", "Use distribution for every pair of terms. (√3 + 2)(√3 − 2) simplifies to 3 − 4 = −1 because the middle terms cancel."]], "trap": "Adding roots is different from multiplying roots. √2 + √3 does not become √5.", "example": "√2(3 + √2) = 3√2 + 2. Multiply √2 by both terms inside the parentheses.", "model": "geo-radical", "day": 3, "constructions": []}, {"id": "geo-rationalize", "title": "Rationalizing Denominators", "desc": "Rationalize a monomial denominator by multiplying numerator and denominator by a suitable radical. For a binomial use its conjugate: (a + √b)(a − √b) = a² − b.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 1 · Day 4", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 1, "parts": [["Rationalizing the denominator", "Rationalize a monomial denominator by multiplying numerator and denominator by a suitable radical. For a binomial use its conjugate: (a + √b)(a − √b) = a² − b."], ["A single radical in the denominator", "Multiply top and bottom by a radical that makes the denominator rational: 2/√3 = 2√3/3. This multiplies the fraction by 1."], ["A binomial in the denominator", "Use the conjugate for a two-term denominator: 1/(√5 + 1) = (√5 − 1)/((√5 + 1)(√5 − 1)) = (√5 − 1)/4."]], "trap": "Adding roots is different from multiplying roots. √2 + √3 does not become √5.", "example": "2/√3 = (2√3)/(√3 × √3) = 2√3/3. Multiplying top and bottom by √3 preserves the value.", "model": "geo-radical", "day": 4, "constructions": []}, {"id": "geo-right-triangles", "title": "Pythagorean Theorem", "desc": "The Pythagorean theorem applies to right triangles: a² + b² = c². The hypotenuse c is opposite the right angle; a and b are the legs.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 1 · Day 5", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 1, "parts": [["Identify the hypotenuse", "The Pythagorean theorem applies to right triangles: a² + b² = c². The hypotenuse c is opposite the right angle; a and b are the legs."], ["Add to find the long side; subtract for a leg", "With legs 6 and 8, c = √(36 + 64) = 10. With hypotenuse 10 and one leg 6, the other is √(100 − 36) = 8. Lengths are positive."], ["Coordinates hide a right triangle", "Horizontal and vertical changes form the legs of a right triangle. Distance = √((x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²). Keep an exact radical unless rounding is requested."]], "trap": "Never use a² + b² = c² on a triangle unless it is a right triangle. c must be the hypotenuse.", "example": "For a square with side 5, d² = 5² + 5² = 50, so its diagonal is √50 = 5√2.", "model": "geo-triangle", "day": 5, "constructions": []}, {"id": "geo-factoring", "title": "Solving Quadratics by Factoring", "desc": "Write the quadratic in standard form ax² + bx + c = 0, with a ≠ 0. Check for a greatest common factor before factoring further.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 2 · Day 1", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 2, "parts": [["Give the equation a zero side", "Write the quadratic in standard form ax² + bx + c = 0, with a ≠ 0. Check for a greatest common factor before factoring further."], ["A zero product gives choices", "The zero-product property says AB = 0 implies A = 0 or B = 0. So x² + x − 6 = (x + 3)(x − 2) = 0 has solutions −3 and 2."], ["Check the original equation", "Substitute each candidate solution into the original equation. Do not lose a solution by dividing by x: x(x − 4) = 0 has both x = 0 and x = 4."]], "trap": "You cannot set each factor equal to zero until the product itself equals zero.", "example": "x² + x − 6 = 0 factors as (x + 3)(x − 2) = 0. The solutions are x = −3 and x = 2.", "model": "geo-quadratic", "day": 1, "constructions": []}, {"id": "geo-formula", "title": "The Quadratic Formula", "desc": "First write ax² + bx + c = 0. The quadratic formula is x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/(2a). Use parentheses when substituting negatives.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 2 · Day 2", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 2, "parts": [["Read a, b, and c with their signs", "First write ax² + bx + c = 0. The quadratic formula is x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/(2a). Use parentheses when substituting negatives."], ["The plus-or-minus makes two paths", "For x² − 2x − 3 = 0, x = (2 ± √16)/2 = (2 ± 4)/2, giving 3 and −1. The entire numerator is divided by 2a."], ["The number under the root is a clue", "The discriminant D = b² − 4ac determines real-root count. D > 0 gives two distinct real roots; D = 0 gives one repeated real root; D < 0 gives no real roots."]], "trap": "The denominator is 2a, not just 2. Squaring a negative b requires parentheses.", "example": "For x² − 2x − 3 = 0, a = 1, b = −2, c = −3. The formula gives x = (2 ± 4)/2 = 3 or −1.", "model": "geo-quadratic", "day": 2, "constructions": []}, {"id": "geo-complete-square", "title": "Completing the Square", "desc": "To complete the square, divide by the leading coefficient if needed, then move the constant. Start with x² + bx = k.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 2 · Day 3", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 2, "parts": [["Make the x² coefficient one", "To complete the square, divide by the leading coefficient if needed, then move the constant. Start with x² + bx = k."], ["Half, then square, then both sides", "Add (b/2)² to both sides of x² + bx = k. Then x² + bx + (b/2)² = (x + b/2)². Keeping both sides balanced preserves equality."], ["Finish with both square-root choices", "After forming a square, use ± when taking square roots to solve the equation. (x + 3)² = 16 gives x = −3 ± 4, or 1 and −7."]], "trap": "Adding to just one side changes the equation. Taking a square root without ± can lose a solution.", "example": "x² + 6x = 7. Add 9 to both sides: (x + 3)² = 16. Thus x = −3 ± 4 = 1 or −7.", "model": "geo-quadratic", "day": 3, "constructions": []}, {"id": "geo-systems", "title": "Systems of Equations", "desc": "A solution to a two-variable system is an ordered pair (x,y) satisfying every equation. Graphically it is an intersection point.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 2 · Day 4", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 2, "parts": [["One answer must fit both rules", "A solution to a two-variable system is an ordered pair (x,y) satisfying every equation. Graphically it is an intersection point."], ["Substitute or eliminate", "Substitution replaces a variable with an equal expression. Elimination multiplies equations as needed and adds or subtracts them to cancel a variable. Solve, then find the other coordinate."], ["Translate a rectangle story", "For area 24 and perimeter 20: LW = 24 and L + W = 10. Substitute W = 10 − L to get L(10 − L) = 24. The dimensions are 4 and 6."]], "trap": "An x-value alone is not a full ordered-pair solution. Check both coordinates in both original equations.", "example": "For y = x + 1 and x + y = 7, substitute: 2x + 1 = 7. Then x = 3 and y = 4. Check both original equations.", "model": "geo-system", "day": 4, "constructions": []}, {"id": "geo-points", "title": "Points, Lines, Segments and Rays", "desc": "A point has no dimensions. A line extends infinitely in both directions. A segment has two endpoints. A ray has one endpoint and extends infinitely in one direction.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 3 · Day 1", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 3, "parts": [["Know what stops and what keeps going", "A point has no dimensions. A line extends infinitely in both directions. A segment has two endpoints. A ray has one endpoint and extends infinitely in one direction."], ["Read coordinates in order", "In (x,y), x gives horizontal position and y vertical position. Quadrants I, II, III, IV have signs (+,+), (−,+), (−,−), (+,−). Points on axes are not in quadrants."], ["Pieces make the whole", "Segment addition: if B lies between A and C, AB + BC = AC. For AB = 2x, BC = x + 3, AC = 18, solve 3x + 3 = 18, so x = 5."]], "trap": "Ray AB and ray BA generally point in different directions. Do not add pieces unless the diagram or text establishes their positions.", "example": "B is between A and C. If AB = 6 and AC = 17, then 6 + BC = 17, giving BC = 11.", "model": "geo-segment", "day": 1, "constructions": ["copy-segment"]}, {"id": "geo-midpoints", "title": "Midpoint", "desc": "If M is the midpoint of AB, AM = MB and AB = 2AM. A segment bisector is a line, ray, or segment passing through that midpoint.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 3 · Day 2", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 3, "parts": [["A midpoint makes equal halves", "If M is the midpoint of AB, AM = MB and AB = 2AM. A segment bisector is a line, ray, or segment passing through that midpoint."], ["Average x, then average y", "M = ((x₁ + x₂)/2, (y₁ + y₂)/2). For a missing endpoint, double the midpoint coordinates and subtract the known endpoint coordinates."], ["Construct the middle with equal arcs", "Use the same compass radius, greater than half the segment length, from both endpoints. Join the two arc intersections. This line crosses the segment at its midpoint and at 90°."]], "trap": "A segment bisector alone guarantees equal halves, not a 90° angle.", "example": "For A(−2,3) and B(4,−3), M = ((−2 + 4)/2, (3 − 3)/2) = (1,0).", "model": "geo-midpoint", "day": 2, "constructions": ["midpoint"]}, {"id": "geo-bisectors", "title": "Segment Bisectors", "desc": "If M is the midpoint of AB, AM = MB and AB = 2AM. A segment bisector is a line, ray, or segment passing through that midpoint.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 3 · Day 3", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 3, "parts": [["A midpoint makes equal halves", "If M is the midpoint of AB, AM = MB and AB = 2AM. A segment bisector is a line, ray, or segment passing through that midpoint."], ["Turn congruent halves into an equation", "A segment bisector passes through the midpoint, so the two halves are congruent. Set their length expressions equal, solve, and substitute to find each requested length."], ["Check what is actually bisected", "State which segment is bisected and identify its midpoint. Do not assume that both intersecting segments bisect each other without evidence."]], "trap": "A segment bisector alone guarantees equal halves, not a 90° angle.", "example": "A bisector creates halves 3x + 1 and x + 9. Set them equal: 3x + 1 = x + 9. Then x = 4 and each half is 13.", "model": "geo-midpoint", "day": 3, "constructions": []}, {"id": "geo-slope", "title": "Slope", "desc": "m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁), when x₂ ≠ x₁. Keep endpoint order consistent. Positive slopes rise left to right; negative slopes fall.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 3 · Day 4", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 3, "parts": [["Slope measures steepness and direction", "m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁), when x₂ ≠ x₁. Keep endpoint order consistent. Positive slopes rise left to right; negative slopes fall."], ["Horizontal is zero; vertical is undefined", "A horizontal line has equation y = k and slope 0. A vertical line has equation x = k and undefined slope. Remember HOY / VUX."], ["Check whether points share a line", "For distinct points, equal slopes from a shared point show collinearity, provided the slopes exist. If all x-coordinates match, the points lie on one vertical line."]], "trap": "Zero slope and undefined slope are different. A vertical line is x = a number, not y = a number.", "example": "Through (1,2) and (3,8), m = (8 − 2)/(3 − 1) = 6/2 = 3. Through two points with the same x-coordinate, slope is undefined.", "model": "geo-slope", "day": 4, "constructions": []}, {"id": "geo-line-equations", "title": "Writing the Equation of a Line", "desc": "In y = mx + b, m is slope and (0,b) is the y-intercept. This form describes nonvertical lines. A vertical line is x = k.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 3 · Day 5", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 3, "parts": [["Use y = mx + b", "In y = mx + b, m is slope and (0,b) is the y-intercept. This form describes nonvertical lines. A vertical line is x = k."], ["Use a point to find b", "Find m, substitute a given point into y = mx + b, solve for b, then write the equation using variables x and y. With m = 3 through (2,7), y = 3x + 1."], ["Check the finished rule", "For y = 3x + 1, the point (2,7) works because 7 = 3(2) + 1. With two given points, check both; also check that the slope matches."]], "trap": "The b is a y-intercept, not whichever y-value appears in the given point.", "example": "Slope 2 through (3,7): 7 = 2(3) + b, so b = 1. The line is y = 2x + 1.", "model": "geo-slope", "day": 5, "constructions": []}, {"id": "geo-angles", "title": "Types of Angles", "desc": "An angle is formed by two rays sharing a vertex. The vertex is the middle letter in a three-letter name. Acute: 0° < θ < 90°; right: 90°; obtuse: 90° < θ < 180°; straight: 180°.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 4 · Day 1", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 4, "parts": [["Name and measure an angle", "An angle is formed by two rays sharing a vertex. The vertex is the middle letter in a three-letter name. Acute: 0° < θ < 90°; right: 90°; obtuse: 90° < θ < 180°; straight: 180°."], ["90, 180, or 360?", "Complementary angles sum to 90°; supplementary angles sum to 180°. A linear pair forms a straight angle, so its angles sum to 180°. Angles around one point sum to 360°."], ["Copy an angle with a compass", "Draw a new base ray. From the original vertex, draw an arc crossing both angle rays. With the same radius, draw an arc from the new vertex. Transfer the distance between the original two arc intersections onto the new arc, then join the new vertex to that mark."]], "trap": "A picture that looks like equal angles is not proof. Use markings, a stated bisector, or a known relationship.", "example": "Two adjacent angles measuring 28° and 47° form a 75° angle. The supplement of 75° is 180° − 75° = 105°.", "model": "geo-angle", "day": 1, "constructions": ["copy-angle"]}, {"id": "geo-angle-bisector", "title": "Angle Bisector", "desc": "An angle bisector divides an angle into two congruent angles. For halves 3x + 5 and 5x − 15, equate them: x = 10, so each half is 35° and the whole is 70°.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 4 · Day 2", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 4, "parts": [["Bisect means two equal angles", "An angle bisector divides an angle into two congruent angles. For halves 3x + 5 and 5x − 15, equate them: x = 10, so each half is 35° and the whole is 70°."], ["Incenter and incircle", "The incenter is the common intersection of the triangle’s three internal angle bisectors. It is equidistant from the sides and is the center of the inscribed circle. Distance to a side is measured perpendicularly."]], "trap": "A picture that looks like equal angles is not proof. Use markings, a stated bisector, or a known relationship.", "example": "An angle bisector makes halves 3x + 5 and 5x − 15. Equate them: x = 10. Each half is 35°, so the whole angle is 70°.", "model": "geo-angle", "day": 2, "constructions": ["angle-bisector", "incircle"]}, {"id": "geo-parallel", "title": "Parallel Lines", "desc": "For two distinct coplanar parallel lines cut by a transversal, corresponding, alternate interior, and alternate exterior angles are congruent. Same-side interior angles are supplementary.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 4 · Day 3", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 4, "parts": [["A transversal crosses two lines", "For two distinct coplanar parallel lines cut by a transversal, corresponding, alternate interior, and alternate exterior angles are congruent. Same-side interior angles are supplementary."], ["Spot matching or adding pairs", "Corresponding angles occupy the same relative position at each intersection. Alternate interior pairs are between the lines on opposite sides of the transversal; alternate exterior pairs are outside on opposite sides."], ["Turn the relationship into an equation", "For parallel lines, corresponding angles 2x + 10 and 70 yield x = 30. Same-side interior angles 2x and 3x yield 5x = 180, so x = 36."]], "trap": "The special equal-angle rules require parallel lines. A transversal alone is not enough.", "example": "For parallel lines, same-side interior angles 2x and 3x satisfy 2x + 3x = 180. Thus x = 36 and the angles are 72° and 108°.", "model": "geo-parallel", "day": 3, "constructions": []}, {"id": "geo-perpendicular", "title": "Constructing Parallel and Perpendicular Lines", "desc": "Distinct nonvertical parallel lines have equal slopes and different intercepts. Distinct vertical lines are parallel to each other.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 4 · Day 4", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 4, "parts": [["Parallel lines share direction", "Distinct nonvertical parallel lines have equal slopes and different intercepts. Distinct vertical lines are parallel to each other."], ["Perpendicular lines make 90°", "Nonvertical perpendicular lines have slopes whose product is −1. Use the negative reciprocal. Horizontal and vertical lines are perpendicular to each other."], ["Find the new line through a point", "A line perpendicular to y = 2x + 1 through (0,3) has slope −1/2 and intercept 3: y = −x/2 + 3. For a vertical answer, use x = k."]], "trap": "Changing only the sign is not enough: the perpendicular slope to 2 is −1/2, not −2.", "example": "A line perpendicular to y = 2x + 1 through (0,3) has slope −1/2, so its equation is y = −x/2 + 3.", "model": "geo-lines", "day": 4, "constructions": ["parallel-corresponding", "parallel-alternate", "perpendicular-on", "perpendicular-off"]}, {"id": "geo-centers", "title": "Perpendicular Bisector", "desc": "A perpendicular bisector passes through a segment’s midpoint at 90°. Every point on it is equidistant from the endpoints. To find its equation, combine the midpoint with a perpendicular slope.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 4 · Day 5", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 4, "parts": [["A perpendicular bisector does two jobs", "A perpendicular bisector passes through a segment’s midpoint at 90°. Every point on it is equidistant from the endpoints. To find its equation, combine the midpoint with a perpendicular slope."], ["Write its equation", "For A(0,0) and B(4,2), midpoint M = (2,1), segment slope = 1/2, and perpendicular slope = −2. Substituting M gives y = −2x + 5."], ["Use equal endpoint distances", "A point on the perpendicular bisector is equidistant from the endpoints. Use PA = PB to solve expressions such as 2x + 3 = x + 8."]], "trap": "Incenter uses angle bisectors and distances to sides. Circumcenter uses perpendicular bisectors and distances to vertices.", "example": "For the segment from (0,0) to (4,2), midpoint M = (2,1) and segment slope is 1/2. Its perpendicular bisector has equation y = −2x + 5.", "model": "geo-centers", "day": 5, "constructions": ["perpendicular-bisector"]}, {"id": "geo-circumcenter", "title": "Circumcenter", "desc": "The circumcenter is the intersection of the side perpendicular bisectors. It is equidistant from the three vertices and is the center of the circumscribed circle. It can lie outside the triangle.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 4 · Day 6", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 4, "parts": [["Circumcenter: equal distance to corners", "The circumcenter is the intersection of the side perpendicular bisectors. It is equidistant from the three vertices and is the center of the circumscribed circle. It can lie outside the triangle."], ["Find it with two perpendicular bisectors", "Two side perpendicular bisectors locate the circumcenter. Use the distance from that point to any vertex as the radius of the circumcircle. All three vertices must lie on the circle."]], "trap": "Incenter uses angle bisectors and distances to sides. Circumcenter uses perpendicular bisectors and distances to vertices.", "example": "A right triangle with vertices (0,0), (6,0), and (0,8) has circumcenter (3,4), the midpoint of its hypotenuse. Each vertex is 5 units from that center.", "model": "geo-centers", "day": 6, "constructions": ["circumcircle"]}, {"id": "geo-angle-strategy", "title": "Unknown Angle Problems", "desc": "Identify the smallest useful relationship: linear pair, vertical angles, triangle sum, full turn, bisector, or parallel-line angle pair. Mark known values before solving.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 4 · Day 7", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 4, "parts": [["Start with one certain relationship", "Identify the smallest useful relationship: linear pair, vertical angles, triangle sum, full turn, bisector, or parallel-line angle pair. Mark known values before solving."], ["Name the unknown and solve", "For triangle angles x, 2x, and 3x, write x + 2x + 3x = 180. Then x = 30°, giving angles 30°, 60°, and 90°."], ["Add a helpful line when it unlocks a clue", "An auxiliary line is a line you add to a diagram to make useful relationships visible. A parallel through a vertex can reveal alternate interior or corresponding angles. Explain the reason for adding it."]], "trap": "If the problem asks for an angle written 3x + 5, finding x is only the first step. Substitute to finish.", "example": "Triangle angles x + 10, 2x, and 80° give 3x + 90 = 180. Then x = 30°, and the three angles are 40°, 60°, and 80°.", "model": "geo-angle", "day": 7, "constructions": []}], "questions": [{"topic": "geo-radicals", "prompt": "Simplify √50.", "options": ["25√2", "5√2", "10√5", "5"], "answer": 1, "why": "50 = 25 × 2, so √50 = 5√2."}, {"topic": "geo-radicals", "prompt": "Which number is irrational?", "options": ["√49", "0.75", "√3", "−4"], "answer": 2, "why": "3 is not a perfect square. √3 cannot be written as a ratio of integers."}, {"topic": "geo-radicals", "prompt": "For any real x, √(x²) equals…", "options": ["x", "−x", "x²", "|x|"], "answer": 3, "why": "The principal square root is nonnegative, so it equals the absolute value of x."}, {"topic": "geo-radical-operations", "prompt": "Simplify √12 + √27.", "options": ["√39", "5√3", "13√3", "3√5"], "answer": 1, "why": "√12 = 2√3 and √27 = 3√3, so the sum is 5√3."}, {"topic": "geo-radical-products", "prompt": "Simplify (√5 + 2)(√5 − 2).", "options": ["1", "9", "√21", "−1"], "answer": 0, "why": "This is a difference of squares: 5 − 4 = 1."}, {"topic": "geo-rationalize", "prompt": "Rationalize 3/√3.", "options": ["3√3", "1/3", "√3", "√3/3"], "answer": 2, "why": "Multiply top and bottom by √3: 3√3/3 = √3."}, {"topic": "geo-right-triangles", "prompt": "A right triangle has legs 5 and 12. Its hypotenuse is…", "options": ["7", "13", "17", "√119"], "answer": 1, "why": "c = √(25 + 144) = √169 = 13."}, {"topic": "geo-right-triangles", "prompt": "Hypotenuse 13, one leg 5. Find the other leg.", "options": ["18", "√194", "8", "12"], "answer": 3, "why": "The missing leg is √(13² − 5²) = √144 = 12."}, {"topic": "geo-right-triangles", "prompt": "Distance from (−1,1) to (2,5)?", "options": ["5", "7", "√7", "25"], "answer": 0, "why": "The changes are 3 and 4, so distance = √(9 + 16) = 5."}, {"topic": "geo-factoring", "prompt": "Solve x² − 9 = 0.", "options": ["x = 9 only", "x = 3 only", "x = −3 or 3", "x = −9 or 9"], "answer": 2, "why": "(x − 3)(x + 3) = 0 gives x = 3 or −3."}, {"topic": "geo-factoring", "prompt": "Solve x(x − 5) = 0.", "options": ["0 and 5", "5 only", "0 and −5", "−5 only"], "answer": 0, "why": "Either x = 0 or x − 5 = 0. Dividing by x would lose the zero solution."}, {"topic": "geo-factoring", "prompt": "Before using the zero-product property, you need…", "options": ["A positive leading coefficient", "A product equal to zero", "A linear equation", "A decimal answer"], "answer": 1, "why": "The property requires a product equal to zero."}, {"topic": "geo-formula", "prompt": "For 3x² − 7x + 2 = 0, b is…", "options": ["7", "−7", "2", "3"], "answer": 1, "why": "The coefficient of x is −7, including its sign."}, {"topic": "geo-formula", "prompt": "A discriminant of −12 means…", "options": ["Two real roots", "One repeated real root", "No real roots", "Twelve real roots"], "answer": 2, "why": "A negative number has no real square root, so the quadratic has no real solutions."}, {"topic": "geo-formula", "prompt": "Solve x² − 2x − 3 = 0.", "options": ["−3 and 1", "3 and −1", "2 and 3", "−2 and −3"], "answer": 1, "why": "The formula gives (2 ± 4)/2, so x = 3 or −1."}, {"topic": "geo-complete-square", "prompt": "What completes x² + 10x + ___?", "options": ["5", "10", "20", "25"], "answer": 3, "why": "Half of 10 is 5, and 5² = 25."}, {"topic": "geo-complete-square", "prompt": "Solve (x + 2)² = 9.", "options": ["1 and −5", "1 only", "5 and −1", "−5 only"], "answer": 0, "why": "x + 2 = ±3, so x = 1 or −5."}, {"topic": "geo-complete-square", "prompt": "To complete x² + 4x = 12, add…", "options": ["4 to the left only", "2 to both sides", "4 to both sides", "16 to both sides"], "answer": 2, "why": "Half of 4 is 2; its square is 4. Add 4 to both sides."}, {"topic": "geo-systems", "prompt": "Solve y = x + 1 and x + y = 7.", "options": ["(4,3)", "(3,4)", "(2,5)", "(7,8)"], "answer": 1, "why": "Substitute: x + x + 1 = 7, so x = 3 and y = 4."}, {"topic": "geo-systems", "prompt": "y = x² and y = 4 have which intersections?", "options": ["(2,4) only", "(−2,4) only", "(−2,4) and (2,4)", "(4,2) and (4,−2)"], "answer": 2, "why": "x² = 4 gives x = ±2; both have y = 4."}, {"topic": "geo-systems", "prompt": "Area 24, perimeter 20: rectangle side lengths?", "options": ["2 and 12", "3 and 8", "4 and 6", "5 and 5"], "answer": 2, "why": "4 × 6 = 24 and 2(4 + 6) = 20."}, {"topic": "geo-points", "prompt": "Which object has exactly one endpoint?", "options": ["Line", "Point", "Ray", "Segment"], "answer": 2, "why": "A ray begins at one endpoint and extends without end in one direction."}, {"topic": "geo-points", "prompt": "Which quadrant contains (−4,2)?", "options": ["I", "II", "III", "IV"], "answer": 1, "why": "Negative x and positive y put the point in quadrant II."}, {"topic": "geo-points", "prompt": "B is between A and C. AB = 7, AC = 19. BC = ?", "options": ["26", "12", "7", "19"], "answer": 1, "why": "AB + BC = AC, so BC = 19 − 7 = 12."}, {"topic": "geo-midpoints", "prompt": "Midpoint of (−2,4) and (6,0)?", "options": ["(4,4)", "(2,2)", "(2,4)", "(−4,2)"], "answer": 1, "why": "Average x: (−2 + 6)/2 = 2; average y: (4 + 0)/2 = 2."}, {"topic": "geo-midpoints", "prompt": "M(3,5) is midpoint; A(1,2). Find B.", "options": ["(2,3)", "(4,7)", "(5,8)", "(6,10)"], "answer": 2, "why": "B = (2·3 − 1, 2·5 − 2) = (5,8)."}, {"topic": "geo-midpoints", "prompt": "Every segment bisector must…", "options": ["Be vertical", "Make a right angle", "Pass through the midpoint", "Be a full line"], "answer": 2, "why": "A bisector passes through the midpoint; perpendicularity is an extra condition."}, {"topic": "geo-slope", "prompt": "Slope through (1,2) and (3,8)?", "options": ["1/3", "3", "6", "−3"], "answer": 1, "why": "m = (8 − 2)/(3 − 1) = 6/2 = 3."}, {"topic": "geo-slope", "prompt": "What is the slope of x = −2?", "options": ["−2", "0", "Undefined", "2"], "answer": 2, "why": "x = −2 is vertical, so its horizontal change is zero."}, {"topic": "geo-slope", "prompt": "A horizontal line through (4,−3) is…", "options": ["x = 4", "y = 4", "x = −3", "y = −3"], "answer": 3, "why": "All points on the horizontal line have the same y-coordinate, −3."}, {"topic": "geo-line-equations", "prompt": "Slope 2 through (3,7). Equation?", "options": ["y = 2x + 7", "y = 2x + 1", "y = 3x + 2", "x = 3"], "answer": 1, "why": "7 = 2(3) + b gives b = 1."}, {"topic": "geo-line-equations", "prompt": "Vertical line through (5,−2)?", "options": ["y = −2", "y = 5", "x = 5", "x = −2"], "answer": 2, "why": "A vertical line keeps x fixed, so x = 5."}, {"topic": "geo-line-equations", "prompt": "y = −3x + 4 has y-intercept…", "options": ["(0,4)", "(4,0)", "(0,−3)", "(−3,0)"], "answer": 0, "why": "Set x = 0: y = 4."}, {"topic": "geo-angles", "prompt": "In ∠PQR, the vertex is…", "options": ["P", "Q", "R", "Any letter"], "answer": 1, "why": "The middle letter Q names the shared endpoint."}, {"topic": "geo-angles", "prompt": "An angle complements 34°. Its measure is…", "options": ["146°", "56°", "34°", "66°"], "answer": 1, "why": "Complementary angles sum to 90°: 90 − 34 = 56."}, {"topic": "geo-angle-bisector", "prompt": "A bisector divides a 126° angle into…", "options": ["Two 63° angles", "Two 126° angles", "36° and 90°", "Two 62° angles"], "answer": 0, "why": "Bisect means two congruent halves, and 126/2 = 63."}, {"topic": "geo-parallel", "prompt": "Parallel lines: an alternate interior partner of 68° measures…", "options": ["112°", "22°", "68°", "136°"], "answer": 2, "why": "Alternate interior angles are congruent when the lines are parallel."}, {"topic": "geo-parallel", "prompt": "Parallel lines: same-side interior angles are 3x and 2x. x = ?", "options": ["18", "36", "45", "90"], "answer": 1, "why": "3x + 2x = 180 gives x = 36."}, {"topic": "geo-parallel", "prompt": "Before using corresponding-angle congruence here, confirm…", "options": ["The lines are parallel", "The angles look equal", "The transversal is horizontal", "All angles are acute"], "answer": 0, "why": "Parallel lines guarantee corresponding angles are congruent."}, {"topic": "geo-perpendicular", "prompt": "Perpendicular slope to 3/4?", "options": ["−3/4", "4/3", "−4/3", "3/4"], "answer": 2, "why": "The negative reciprocal is −4/3; their product is −1."}, {"topic": "geo-perpendicular", "prompt": "Parallel to y = −2x + 5 through (1,1)?", "options": ["y = 2x − 1", "y = −2x + 3", "y = x + 1", "x = 1"], "answer": 1, "why": "Use slope −2. Then 1 = −2(1) + b, so b = 3."}, {"topic": "geo-perpendicular", "prompt": "A line perpendicular to a horizontal line is…", "options": ["Horizontal", "Vertical", "Always slope 1", "Always slope −1"], "answer": 1, "why": "A vertical line meets a horizontal line at 90°."}, {"topic": "geo-angle-bisector", "prompt": "Which point is equidistant from all three triangle sides?", "options": ["Circumcenter", "Incenter", "Any vertex", "Any midpoint"], "answer": 1, "why": "The incenter lies on the angle bisectors and is equidistant from the sides."}, {"topic": "geo-circumcenter", "prompt": "A circumcircle passes through…", "options": ["All three vertices", "All three side midpoints", "Only one vertex", "The incenter only"], "answer": 0, "why": "The circumcenter is equidistant from the vertices, so its circle passes through all three."}, {"topic": "geo-centers", "prompt": "Perpendicular bisector of (0,0) to (6,0)?", "options": ["y = 3", "x = 6", "y = 0", "x = 3"], "answer": 3, "why": "The midpoint is (3,0); the segment is horizontal, so its perpendicular bisector is vertical, x = 3."}, {"topic": "geo-angle-strategy", "prompt": "Triangle angles 45°, 65°, and θ. θ = ?", "options": ["70°", "80°", "110°", "250°"], "answer": 0, "why": "Triangle angles sum to 180°: 180 − 45 − 65 = 70."}, {"topic": "geo-angle-strategy", "prompt": "Angles around a point total…", "options": ["90°", "180°", "270°", "360°"], "answer": 3, "why": "One full turn is 360°."}, {"topic": "geo-angle-strategy", "prompt": "x = 20 and an angle is 3x + 5 degrees. The angle is…", "options": ["20°", "60°", "65°", "25°"], "answer": 2, "why": "Substitute x = 20: 3(20) + 5 = 65°."}, {"topic": "geo-radical-operations", "prompt": "Simplify 7√5 − 2√5.", "options": ["5", "5√5", "√5", "9√5"], "answer": 1, "why": "Subtract the coefficients; keep the common radical: (7 − 2)√5 = 5√5."}, {"topic": "geo-radical-operations", "prompt": "Which pair can be combined directly by adding coefficients?", "options": ["√2 and √3", "2√7 and 5√7", "√5 and 5", "√2 and 2"], "answer": 1, "why": "The second pair has the same simplified radicand, 7."}, {"topic": "geo-radical-products", "prompt": "Simplify √6 × √24.", "options": ["√30", "6", "12", "24"], "answer": 2, "why": "√(6 × 24) = √144 = 12."}, {"topic": "geo-radical-products", "prompt": "Simplify √75 ÷ √3.", "options": ["5", "25", "√78", "√72"], "answer": 0, "why": "√(75/3) = √25 = 5."}, {"topic": "geo-rationalize", "prompt": "Rationalize 1/(√5 + 1).", "options": ["(√5 + 1)/6", "(√5 − 1)/4", "√5 − 1", "1/6"], "answer": 1, "why": "Multiply numerator and denominator by √5 − 1. The denominator becomes 5 − 1 = 4."}, {"topic": "geo-rationalize", "prompt": "The conjugate of 3 − √2 is…", "options": ["−3 + √2", "3 + √2", "√3 − 2", "3 − √2"], "answer": 1, "why": "A conjugate changes the sign between the same two terms."}, {"topic": "geo-bisectors", "prompt": "A bisector makes segment halves 3x + 2 and x + 10. Find x.", "options": ["2", "4", "6", "8"], "answer": 1, "why": "Set equal: 3x + 2 = x + 10, so 2x = 8 and x = 4."}, {"topic": "geo-bisectors", "prompt": "A line bisects AB at M. If AM = 9, AB = ?", "options": ["4.5", "9", "18", "27"], "answer": 2, "why": "AM = MB = 9, so AB = 18."}, {"topic": "geo-bisectors", "prompt": "Does a segment bisector always meet the segment at 90°?", "options": ["Yes", "Only if the segment is horizontal", "No", "Only on a grid"], "answer": 2, "why": "It must pass through the midpoint. Perpendicularity is an additional condition."}, {"topic": "geo-angles", "prompt": "An angle of 125° is…", "options": ["Acute", "Right", "Obtuse", "Straight"], "answer": 2, "why": "Obtuse angles are greater than 90° and less than 180°."}, {"topic": "geo-angle-bisector", "prompt": "Angle halves are 2x + 5 and 3x − 10. Find x.", "options": ["5", "10", "15", "20"], "answer": 2, "why": "Bisected angles are equal: 2x + 5 = 3x − 10 gives x = 15."}, {"topic": "geo-centers", "prompt": "Every point on a perpendicular bisector is…", "options": ["Equidistant from the segment endpoints", "A vertex", "Inside a triangle", "On the original segment"], "answer": 0, "why": "The perpendicular bisector is the set of points equally distant from both endpoints."}, {"topic": "geo-centers", "prompt": "Perpendicular bisector of the segment (0,0) to (4,2)?", "options": ["y = 2x + 1", "y = −2x + 5", "y = x/2", "x = 4"], "answer": 1, "why": "Midpoint (2,1), perpendicular slope −2: y − 1 = −2(x − 2), so y = −2x + 5."}, {"topic": "geo-circumcenter", "prompt": "The circumcenter is the intersection of…", "options": ["Medians", "Angle bisectors", "Side perpendicular bisectors", "Triangle sides"], "answer": 2, "why": "Side perpendicular bisectors meet at a point equidistant from all three vertices."}, {"topic": "geo-circumcenter", "prompt": "Can a circumcenter be outside its triangle?", "options": ["Yes, for an obtuse triangle", "No, never", "Only for an equilateral triangle", "Only for an acute triangle"], "answer": 0, "why": "An obtuse triangle has its circumcenter outside; the incenter stays inside."}], "cards": [], "reading": {"geo-radicals": {"easy": {"parts": [["Square roots and number types", "A square root reverses squaring. Since 5² = 25, √25 = 5. The radical symbol gives the nonnegative root; solving x² = 25 gives both 5 and −5."], ["Simplify using a perfect-square factor", "Simplify √72 by factoring 72 as 36 × 2. Since √36 = 6, √72 = 6√2. Use the largest perfect-square factor to reduce the work."], ["Variable radicals and absolute value", "For any real x, √(x²) = |x| because a principal square root cannot be negative. If x is known to be nonnegative, |x| = x."]], "trap": "√(a + b) is not generally √a + √b. A radical symbol alone gives the nonnegative root.", "example": "√98 = √(49 × 2) = 7√2. The factor 49 is a perfect square.", "desc": "A square root reverses squaring. Since 5² = 25, √25 = 5. The radical symbol gives the nonnegative root; solving x² = 25 gives both 5 and −5."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Square roots and number types", "A square root reverses squaring. √a means the principal, nonnegative square root. √25 = 5, while solving x² = 25 gives x = ±5."], ["Simplify using a perfect-square factor", "To simplify √72, find a perfect-square factor: √(36 × 2) = 6√2. Keep factoring until no perfect square greater than 1 remains inside."], ["Variable radicals and absolute value", "√(x²) = |x|, not always x. For example, if x = −3, the root is 3. If a problem states x ≥ 0, then √(x²) = x."]], "trap": "√(a + b) is not generally √a + √b. A radical symbol alone gives the nonnegative root.", "example": "√98 = √(49 × 2) = 7√2. The factor 49 is a perfect square.", "desc": "A square root reverses squaring. √a means the principal, nonnegative square root. √25 = 5, while solving x² = 25 gives x = ±5."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Square roots and number types", "For a ≥ 0, √a is the unique nonnegative real number whose square is a. A rational number is a ratio of integers; √2 is irrational, but √9 = 3 is rational."], ["Simplify using a perfect-square factor", "For nonnegative a and b, √(ab) = √a √b. Extract the greatest perfect-square factor: √72 = √(36·2) = 6√2. This is an exact value; a rounded decimal is an approximation."], ["Variable radicals and absolute value", "Because the principal root is nonnegative, √(x²) = |x| for real x. For x ≥ 0, √(18x²) = 3x√2; without that assumption it is 3|x|√2."]], "trap": "√(a + b) is not generally √a + √b. A radical symbol alone gives the nonnegative root.", "example": "√98 = √(49 × 2) = 7√2. The factor 49 is a perfect square.", "desc": "For a ≥ 0, √a is the unique nonnegative real number whose square is a. A rational number is a ratio of integers; √2 is irrational, but √9 = 3 is rational."}}, "geo-radical-operations": {"easy": {"parts": [["Like radicals", "Add or subtract radicals only when their simplified radicands match. For example, 3√2 + 4√2 = 7√2. The radicand stays 2."], ["Simplify before combining", "√8 + √18 = 2√2 + 3√2 = 5√2. Simplifying first reveals that the terms are like radicals."], ["Handle subtraction carefully", "For 4√3 − (√3 + 2√5), subtract both terms inside the parentheses. The result is 3√3 − 2√5."]], "trap": "Adding roots is different from multiplying roots. √2 + √3 does not become √5.", "example": "√20 + √45 = 2√5 + 3√5 = 5√5. Simplify before combining coefficients.", "desc": "Add or subtract radicals only when their simplified radicands match. For example, 3√2 + 4√2 = 7√2. The radicand stays 2."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Like radicals", "Combine coefficients only when the simplified radicands match. √8 + √18 = 2√2 + 3√2 = 5√2."], ["Simplify before combining", "Simplify every radical before combining terms. For √8 + √18, extract square factors to get 2√2 + 3√2 = 5√2."], ["Handle subtraction carefully", "Distribute a subtraction sign to every term it affects. 4√3 − (√3 + 2√5) = 4√3 − √3 − 2√5 = 3√3 − 2√5."]], "trap": "Adding roots is different from multiplying roots. √2 + √3 does not become √5.", "example": "√20 + √45 = 2√5 + 3√5 = 5√5. Simplify before combining coefficients.", "desc": "Combine coefficients only when the simplified radicands match. √8 + √18 = 2√2 + 3√2 = 5√2."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Like radicals", "Like radicals have the same index and radicand after simplification. Add or subtract their coefficients. Simplification must precede deciding whether radicals are like."], ["Simplify before combining", "Rewrite radicals in simplest form before identifying like radical factors. Combine only coefficients of matching simplified radicals."], ["Handle subtraction carefully", "Subtraction is addition of the opposite expression. Apply the negative sign across all terms, then combine like radicals; unlike radicals remain separate."]], "trap": "Adding roots is different from multiplying roots. √2 + √3 does not become √5.", "example": "√20 + √45 = 2√5 + 3√5 = 5√5. Simplify before combining coefficients.", "desc": "Like radicals have the same index and radicand after simplification. Add or subtract their coefficients. Simplification must precede deciding whether radicals are like."}}, "geo-radical-products": {"easy": {"parts": [["Multiply every needed pair", "Multiply the coefficients and multiply the radicands, then simplify. √3 × √12 = √36 = 6. Use distribution when a factor has more than one term."], ["Divide and simplify", "For √48 ÷ √3, divide inside the roots: √(48/3) = √16 = 4. The denominator cannot be zero."], ["Multiply two binomials", "(√3 + 2)(√3 − 2) = 3 − 2√3 + 2√3 − 4 = −1. Multiply all four pairs, then combine terms."]], "trap": "Adding roots is different from multiplying roots. √2 + √3 does not become √5.", "example": "√2(3 + √2) = 3√2 + 2. Multiply √2 by both terms inside the parentheses.", "desc": "Multiply the coefficients and multiply the radicands, then simplify. √3 × √12 = √36 = 6. Use distribution when a factor has more than one term."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Multiply every needed pair", "Multiply coefficients and radicands, then simplify. Use distribution: √2(3 + √2) = 3√2 + 2. For two binomials, multiply all four pairs."], ["Divide and simplify", "For a ≥ 0 and b > 0, √a/√b = √(a/b). Simplify the fraction, then the radical. For example, √48/√3 = 4."], ["Multiply two binomials", "Use distribution for every pair of terms. (√3 + 2)(√3 − 2) simplifies to 3 − 4 = −1 because the middle terms cancel."]], "trap": "Adding roots is different from multiplying roots. √2 + √3 does not become √5.", "example": "√2(3 + √2) = 3√2 + 2. Multiply √2 by both terms inside the parentheses.", "desc": "Multiply coefficients and radicands, then simplify. Use distribution: √2(3 + √2) = 3√2 + 2. For two binomials, multiply all four pairs."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Multiply every needed pair", "For real square roots with nonnegative radicands, multiply using √a√b = √(ab). Distribution gives (√3 + 2)(√3 − 2) = 3 − 4 = −1."], ["Divide and simplify", "The quotient property requires a nonnegative numerator radicand and positive denominator radicand in real-number work. Apply distribution to polynomial products, and simplify all resulting radicals."], ["Multiply two binomials", "The product of conjugates is a difference of squares. Distribution gives (a + b)(a − b) = a² − b², including when one or both terms are radicals."]], "trap": "Adding roots is different from multiplying roots. √2 + √3 does not become √5.", "example": "√2(3 + √2) = 3√2 + 2. Multiply √2 by both terms inside the parentheses.", "desc": "For real square roots with nonnegative radicands, multiply using √a√b = √(ab). Distribution gives (√3 + 2)(√3 − 2) = 3 − 4 = −1."}}, "geo-rationalize": {"easy": {"parts": [["Rationalizing the denominator", "Rationalizing rewrites a fraction without a radical in its denominator. Multiply the numerator and denominator by the same nonzero expression so the value stays unchanged."], ["A single radical in the denominator", "For 2/√3, multiply the numerator and denominator by √3. The result is 2√3/3."], ["A binomial in the denominator", "For 1/(√5 + 1), multiply the numerator and denominator by √5 − 1. The denominator becomes 5 − 1 = 4."]], "trap": "Adding roots is different from multiplying roots. √2 + √3 does not become √5.", "example": "2/√3 = (2√3)/(√3 × √3) = 2√3/3. Multiplying top and bottom by √3 preserves the value.", "desc": "Rationalizing rewrites a fraction without a radical in its denominator. Multiply the numerator and denominator by the same nonzero expression so the value stays unchanged."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Rationalizing the denominator", "Rationalize a monomial denominator by multiplying numerator and denominator by a suitable radical. For a binomial use its conjugate: (a + √b)(a − √b) = a² − b."], ["A single radical in the denominator", "Multiply top and bottom by a radical that makes the denominator rational: 2/√3 = 2√3/3. This multiplies the fraction by 1."], ["A binomial in the denominator", "Use the conjugate for a two-term denominator: 1/(√5 + 1) = (√5 − 1)/((√5 + 1)(√5 − 1)) = (√5 − 1)/4."]], "trap": "Adding roots is different from multiplying roots. √2 + √3 does not become √5.", "example": "2/√3 = (2√3)/(√3 × √3) = 2√3/3. Multiplying top and bottom by √3 preserves the value.", "desc": "Rationalize a monomial denominator by multiplying numerator and denominator by a suitable radical. For a binomial use its conjugate: (a + √b)(a − √b) = a² − b."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Rationalizing the denominator", "Multiplying by a nonzero expression divided by itself preserves a fraction. For 1/(√5 + 1), use (√5 − 1)/(√5 − 1), giving (√5 − 1)/4. The original denominator must be nonzero."], ["A single radical in the denominator", "Choose a radical factor that converts the denominator radicand to a perfect square. Apply the same nonzero factor to numerator and denominator to preserve equivalence."], ["A binomial in the denominator", "The conjugate removes the radical cross terms through the difference-of-squares identity. Preserve any original nonzero-denominator restrictions and simplify the resulting fraction."]], "trap": "Adding roots is different from multiplying roots. √2 + √3 does not become √5.", "example": "2/√3 = (2√3)/(√3 × √3) = 2√3/3. Multiplying top and bottom by √3 preserves the value.", "desc": "Multiplying by a nonzero expression divided by itself preserves a fraction. For 1/(√5 + 1), use (√5 − 1)/(√5 − 1), giving (√5 − 1)/4. The original denominator must be nonzero."}}, "geo-right-triangles": {"easy": {"parts": [["Identify the hypotenuse", "In a right triangle, the hypotenuse is opposite the 90° angle. The other sides are the legs. The Pythagorean theorem is a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse."], ["Add to find the long side; subtract for a leg", "For legs 6 and 8, c² = 36 + 64 = 100, so c = 10. To find a missing leg, subtract the known leg’s square from the hypotenuse’s square before taking the square root."], ["Coordinates hide a right triangle", "From (1,2) to (4,6), the horizontal change is 3 and vertical change is 4. These form perpendicular legs, so the distance is √(3² + 4²) = 5."]], "trap": "Never use a² + b² = c² on a triangle unless it is a right triangle. c must be the hypotenuse.", "example": "For a square with side 5, d² = 5² + 5² = 50, so its diagonal is √50 = 5√2.", "desc": "In a right triangle, the hypotenuse is opposite the 90° angle. The other sides are the legs. The Pythagorean theorem is a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Identify the hypotenuse", "The Pythagorean theorem applies to right triangles: a² + b² = c². The hypotenuse c is opposite the right angle; a and b are the legs."], ["Add to find the long side; subtract for a leg", "With legs 6 and 8, c = √(36 + 64) = 10. With hypotenuse 10 and one leg 6, the other is √(100 − 36) = 8. Lengths are positive."], ["Coordinates hide a right triangle", "Horizontal and vertical changes form the legs of a right triangle. Distance = √((x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²). Keep an exact radical unless rounding is requested."]], "trap": "Never use a² + b² = c² on a triangle unless it is a right triangle. c must be the hypotenuse.", "example": "For a square with side 5, d² = 5² + 5² = 50, so its diagonal is √50 = 5√2.", "desc": "The Pythagorean theorem applies to right triangles: a² + b² = c². The hypotenuse c is opposite the right angle; a and b are the legs."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Identify the hypotenuse", "In a Euclidean right triangle, the sum of the squares of the leg lengths equals the square of the hypotenuse. Identify c from the right-angle mark, not the drawing’s orientation."], ["Add to find the long side; subtract for a leg", "Solve for the required squared length before taking the principal root. Reject algebraic roots that produce nonpositive side lengths. For a square of side s, diagonal d = s√2 for s > 0."], ["Coordinates hide a right triangle", "The distance formula follows from the Pythagorean theorem using perpendicular coordinate changes. Reversing both endpoint orders leaves the squared differences unchanged."]], "trap": "Never use a² + b² = c² on a triangle unless it is a right triangle. c must be the hypotenuse.", "example": "For a square with side 5, d² = 5² + 5² = 50, so its diagonal is √50 = 5√2.", "desc": "In a Euclidean right triangle, the sum of the squares of the leg lengths equals the square of the hypotenuse. Identify c from the right-angle mark, not the drawing’s orientation."}}, "geo-factoring": {"easy": {"parts": [["Give the equation a zero side", "Write the equation with zero on one side and terms in descending powers. Factor out a common factor first, if there is one."], ["A zero product gives choices", "If a product equals zero, at least one factor equals zero. For (x − 2)(x + 3) = 0, solve x − 2 = 0 or x + 3 = 0. The solutions are 2 and −3."], ["Check the original equation", "Substitute each solution into the original equation. Do not divide by a variable that could be zero: x(x − 4) = 0 has solutions 0 and 4."]], "trap": "You cannot set each factor equal to zero until the product itself equals zero.", "example": "x² + x − 6 = 0 factors as (x + 3)(x − 2) = 0. The solutions are x = −3 and x = 2.", "desc": "Write the equation with zero on one side and terms in descending powers. Factor out a common factor first, if there is one."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Give the equation a zero side", "Write the quadratic in standard form ax² + bx + c = 0, with a ≠ 0. Check for a greatest common factor before factoring further."], ["A zero product gives choices", "The zero-product property says AB = 0 implies A = 0 or B = 0. So x² + x − 6 = (x + 3)(x − 2) = 0 has solutions −3 and 2."], ["Check the original equation", "Substitute each candidate solution into the original equation. Do not lose a solution by dividing by x: x(x − 4) = 0 has both x = 0 and x = 4."]], "trap": "You cannot set each factor equal to zero until the product itself equals zero.", "example": "x² + x − 6 = 0 factors as (x + 3)(x − 2) = 0. The solutions are x = −3 and x = 2.", "desc": "Write the quadratic in standard form ax² + bx + c = 0, with a ≠ 0. Check for a greatest common factor before factoring further."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Give the equation a zero side", "Standard form makes coefficients and factor structure visible. An equivalent equation may be obtained by adding, subtracting, or dividing both sides by a nonzero constant."], ["A zero product gives choices", "After factoring completely, set each variable factor equal to zero and solve. The zero-product property applies to products equal to zero, not sums or products equal to another number."], ["Check the original equation", "Verification checks the original relationship and any restrictions from a context. A quadratic can have two, one, or no real roots; repeated factors yield a repeated root."]], "trap": "You cannot set each factor equal to zero until the product itself equals zero.", "example": "x² + x − 6 = 0 factors as (x + 3)(x − 2) = 0. The solutions are x = −3 and x = 2.", "desc": "Standard form makes coefficients and factor structure visible. An equivalent equation may be obtained by adding, subtracting, or dividing both sides by a nonzero constant."}}, "geo-formula": {"easy": {"parts": [["Read a, b, and c with their signs", "First write ax² + bx + c = 0. In 2x² − 3x − 5 = 0, a = 2, b = −3, and c = −5. Keep the signs with the coefficients."], ["The plus-or-minus makes two paths", "Use x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/(2a). Calculate once with + and once with −. Divide the entire numerator by 2a."], ["The number under the root is a clue", "The discriminant is b² − 4ac. A positive result gives two real solutions, zero gives one repeated solution, and a negative result gives no real solutions."]], "trap": "The denominator is 2a, not just 2. Squaring a negative b requires parentheses.", "example": "For x² − 2x − 3 = 0, a = 1, b = −2, c = −3. The formula gives x = (2 ± 4)/2 = 3 or −1.", "desc": "First write ax² + bx + c = 0. In 2x² − 3x − 5 = 0, a = 2, b = −3, and c = −5. Keep the signs with the coefficients."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Read a, b, and c with their signs", "First write ax² + bx + c = 0. The quadratic formula is x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/(2a). Use parentheses when substituting negatives."], ["The plus-or-minus makes two paths", "For x² − 2x − 3 = 0, x = (2 ± √16)/2 = (2 ± 4)/2, giving 3 and −1. The entire numerator is divided by 2a."], ["The number under the root is a clue", "The discriminant D = b² − 4ac determines real-root count. D > 0 gives two distinct real roots; D = 0 gives one repeated real root; D < 0 gives no real roots."]], "trap": "The denominator is 2a, not just 2. Squaring a negative b requires parentheses.", "example": "For x² − 2x − 3 = 0, a = 1, b = −2, c = −3. The formula gives x = (2 ± 4)/2 = 3 or −1.", "desc": "First write ax² + bx + c = 0. The quadratic formula is x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/(2a). Use parentheses when substituting negatives."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Read a, b, and c with their signs", "For a ≠ 0, the formula solves any quadratic over the complex numbers; here we focus on real roots. Coefficients must be identified from standard form, including their signs."], ["The plus-or-minus makes two paths", "Evaluate the discriminant first, simplify its square root, then reduce the full fraction. If the discriminant is zero, the two formula branches coincide."], ["The number under the root is a clue", "For real coefficients, D classifies the real roots. With integer coefficients, a positive perfect-square discriminant produces rational roots; a positive nonsquare discriminant produces irrational roots."]], "trap": "The denominator is 2a, not just 2. Squaring a negative b requires parentheses.", "example": "For x² − 2x − 3 = 0, a = 1, b = −2, c = −3. The formula gives x = (2 ± 4)/2 = 3 or −1.", "desc": "For a ≠ 0, the formula solves any quadratic over the complex numbers; here we focus on real roots. Coefficients must be identified from standard form, including their signs."}}, "geo-complete-square": {"easy": {"parts": [["Make the x² coefficient one", "If the coefficient of x² is not 1, divide every term by it. Then move the constant term to the other side."], ["Half, then square, then both sides", "For x² + 6x, half of 6 is 3 and 3² = 9. Add 9 to both sides so the left becomes (x + 3)²."], ["Finish with both square-root choices", "x² + 6x = 7 becomes (x + 3)² = 16. Then x + 3 = ±4, giving x = 1 or x = −7."]], "trap": "Adding to just one side changes the equation. Taking a square root without ± can lose a solution.", "example": "x² + 6x = 7. Add 9 to both sides: (x + 3)² = 16. Thus x = −3 ± 4 = 1 or −7.", "desc": "If the coefficient of x² is not 1, divide every term by it. Then move the constant term to the other side."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Make the x² coefficient one", "To complete the square, divide by the leading coefficient if needed, then move the constant. Start with x² + bx = k."], ["Half, then square, then both sides", "Add (b/2)² to both sides of x² + bx = k. Then x² + bx + (b/2)² = (x + b/2)². Keeping both sides balanced preserves equality."], ["Finish with both square-root choices", "After forming a square, use ± when taking square roots to solve the equation. (x + 3)² = 16 gives x = −3 ± 4, or 1 and −7."]], "trap": "Adding to just one side changes the equation. Taking a square root without ± can lose a solution.", "example": "x² + 6x = 7. Add 9 to both sides: (x + 3)² = 16. Thus x = −3 ± 4 = 1 or −7.", "desc": "To complete the square, divide by the leading coefficient if needed, then move the constant. Start with x² + bx = k."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Make the x² coefficient one", "For ax² + bx + c = 0 with a ≠ 0, dividing by a normalizes the quadratic term. Isolate the variable terms before forming a perfect-square trinomial."], ["Half, then square, then both sides", "The identity (x + h)² = x² + 2hx + h² explains the method: h = b/2 after normalization. Fractional values of h are allowed."], ["Finish with both square-root choices", "Solve the resulting equation using the square-root property and isolate x. The principal radical √16 is 4, but an equation u² = 16 has u = ±4."]], "trap": "Adding to just one side changes the equation. Taking a square root without ± can lose a solution.", "example": "x² + 6x = 7. Add 9 to both sides: (x + 3)² = 16. Thus x = −3 ± 4 = 1 or −7.", "desc": "For ax² + bx + c = 0 with a ≠ 0, dividing by a normalizes the quadratic term. Isolate the variable terms before forming a perfect-square trinomial."}}, "geo-systems": {"easy": {"parts": [["One answer must fit both rules", "A solution to a system must satisfy both equations. On a graph, the solutions are the intersection points."], ["Substitute or eliminate", "For substitution, replace a variable with an equal expression from the other equation. For elimination, combine equations so one variable cancels. Then solve for both coordinates."], ["Translate a rectangle story", "For a rectangle, area = LW and perimeter = 2L + 2W. These form two equations for the same dimensions. Keep only positive side lengths."]], "trap": "An x-value alone is not a full ordered-pair solution. Check both coordinates in both original equations.", "example": "For y = x + 1 and x + y = 7, substitute: 2x + 1 = 7. Then x = 3 and y = 4. Check both original equations.", "desc": "A solution to a system must satisfy both equations. On a graph, the solutions are the intersection points."}, "medium": {"parts": [["One answer must fit both rules", "A solution to a two-variable system is an ordered pair (x,y) satisfying every equation. Graphically it is an intersection point."], ["Substitute or eliminate", "Substitution replaces a variable with an equal expression. Elimination multiplies equations as needed and adds or subtracts them to cancel a variable. Solve, then find the other coordinate."], ["Translate a rectangle story", "For area 24 and perimeter 20: LW = 24 and L + W = 10. Substitute W = 10 − L to get L(10 − L) = 24. The dimensions are 4 and 6."]], "trap": "An x-value alone is not a full ordered-pair solution. Check both coordinates in both original equations.", "example": "For y = x + 1 and x + y = 7, substitute: 2x + 1 = 7. Then x = 3 and y = 4. Check both original equations.", "desc": "A solution to a two-variable system is an ordered pair (x,y) satisfying every equation. Graphically it is an intersection point."}, "expert": {"parts": [["One answer must fit both rules", "The solution set is the intersection of the equations’ solution sets. A linear–quadratic system can have zero, one, or two real intersection points."], ["Substitute or eliminate", "Equivalent substitution and linear combinations reduce the number of unknowns. In a linear–quadratic system, substitution often produces a quadratic; each real root must be paired with its matching y-value."], ["Translate a rectangle story", "Use consistent units and positive dimensions. The roots 4 and 6 of L² − 10L + 24 = 0 describe the same rectangle when length and width labels are exchanged."]], "trap": "An x-value alone is not a full ordered-pair solution. Check both coordinates in both original equations.", "example": "For y = x + 1 and x + y = 7, substitute: 2x + 1 = 7. Then x = 3 and y = 4. Check both original equations.", "desc": "The solution set is the intersection of the equations’ solution sets. A linear–quadratic system can have zero, one, or two real intersection points."}}, "geo-points": {"easy": {"parts": [["Know what stops and what keeps going", "A point is a location. A line extends in both directions; a segment has two endpoints. A ray has one endpoint and extends in one direction."], ["Read coordinates in order", "In (x,y), x is the horizontal coordinate and y is the vertical coordinate. To plot (−2,3), move left 2 and up 3."], ["Pieces make the whole", "If B lies between A and C, AB + BC = AC. If AC = 15 and AB = 6, then BC = 9. Use a compass to transfer a segment length without measuring it with a ruler."]], "trap": "Ray AB and ray BA generally point in different directions. Do not add pieces unless the diagram or text establishes their positions.", "example": "B is between A and C. If AB = 6 and AC = 17, then 6 + BC = 17, giving BC = 11.", "desc": "A point is a location. A line extends in both directions; a segment has two endpoints. A ray has one endpoint and extends in one direction."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Know what stops and what keeps going", "A point has no dimensions. A line extends infinitely in both directions. A segment has two endpoints. A ray has one endpoint and extends infinitely in one direction."], ["Read coordinates in order", "In (x,y), x gives horizontal position and y vertical position. Quadrants I, II, III, IV have signs (+,+), (−,+), (−,−), (+,−). Points on axes are not in quadrants."], ["Pieces make the whole", "Segment addition: if B lies between A and C, AB + BC = AC. For AB = 2x, BC = x + 3, AC = 18, solve 3x + 3 = 18, so x = 5."]], "trap": "Ray AB and ray BA generally point in different directions. Do not add pieces unless the diagram or text establishes their positions.", "example": "B is between A and C. If AB = 6 and AC = 17, then 6 + BC = 17, giving BC = 11.", "desc": "A point has no dimensions. A line extends infinitely in both directions. A segment has two endpoints. A ray has one endpoint and extends infinitely in one direction."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Know what stops and what keeps going", "Points identify positions; lines and rays have infinite extent, while segments have finite length. Name a ray with its endpoint first: ray AB begins at A and passes through B."], ["Read coordinates in order", "The Cartesian plane uses perpendicular x- and y-axes. Ordered pairs locate points uniquely; reversing coordinates generally gives a different point."], ["Pieces make the whole", "Between-ness is required for this segment-addition equation. Segment notation denotes the geometric set; AB without a segment bar commonly denotes its length. Copy a length with a compass by transferring the endpoint distance."]], "trap": "Ray AB and ray BA generally point in different directions. Do not add pieces unless the diagram or text establishes their positions.", "example": "B is between A and C. If AB = 6 and AC = 17, then 6 + BC = 17, giving BC = 11.", "desc": "Points identify positions; lines and rays have infinite extent, while segments have finite length. Name a ray with its endpoint first: ray AB begins at A and passes through B."}}, "geo-midpoints": {"easy": {"parts": [["A midpoint makes equal halves", "A midpoint divides a segment into two congruent segments. A segment bisector passes through that midpoint; it does not have to be perpendicular."], ["Average x, then average y", "Average the x-coordinates and then the y-coordinates. The midpoint of (0,2) and (6,8) is (3,5). To find a missing endpoint, double the midpoint and subtract the known endpoint."], ["Construct the middle with equal arcs", "Use equal compass openings greater than half the segment length. Draw intersecting arcs from both endpoints. Connect the two arc intersections to locate the midpoint."]], "trap": "A segment bisector alone guarantees equal halves, not a 90° angle.", "example": "For A(−2,3) and B(4,−3), M = ((−2 + 4)/2, (3 − 3)/2) = (1,0).", "desc": "A midpoint divides a segment into two congruent segments. A segment bisector passes through that midpoint; it does not have to be perpendicular."}, "medium": {"parts": [["A midpoint makes equal halves", "If M is the midpoint of AB, AM = MB and AB = 2AM. A segment bisector is a line, ray, or segment passing through that midpoint."], ["Average x, then average y", "M = ((x₁ + x₂)/2, (y₁ + y₂)/2). For a missing endpoint, double the midpoint coordinates and subtract the known endpoint coordinates."], ["Construct the middle with equal arcs", "Use the same compass radius, greater than half the segment length, from both endpoints. Join the two arc intersections. This line crosses the segment at its midpoint and at 90°."]], "trap": "A segment bisector alone guarantees equal halves, not a 90° angle.", "example": "For A(−2,3) and B(4,−3), M = ((−2 + 4)/2, (3 − 3)/2) = (1,0).", "desc": "If M is the midpoint of AB, AM = MB and AB = 2AM. A segment bisector is a line, ray, or segment passing through that midpoint."}, "expert": {"parts": [["A midpoint makes equal halves", "A midpoint partitions a segment into two congruent segments. A segment bisector need not be perpendicular; passing through the midpoint is the defining condition."], ["Average x, then average y", "The midpoint formula averages coordinate components independently. If M is the midpoint of AB, then B = (2Mₓ − Aₓ, 2Mᵧ − Aᵧ)."], ["Construct the middle with equal arcs", "Equal-radius intersecting circles centered at A and B determine two points equidistant from A and B. Their connecting line is the perpendicular bisector. A radius at or below half the segment length does not give two distinct intersections."]], "trap": "A segment bisector alone guarantees equal halves, not a 90° angle.", "example": "For A(−2,3) and B(4,−3), M = ((−2 + 4)/2, (3 − 3)/2) = (1,0).", "desc": "A midpoint partitions a segment into two congruent segments. A segment bisector need not be perpendicular; passing through the midpoint is the defining condition."}}, "geo-bisectors": {"easy": {"parts": [["A midpoint makes equal halves", "A midpoint is the exact middle of a segment. The two little lengths are equal."], ["Turn congruent halves into an equation", "If a bisector creates lengths 4x − 1 and 2x + 7, set them equal. Then 2x = 8, so x = 4 and each half is 15."], ["Check what is actually bisected", "Look for the segment named in the problem. A line crossing two segments might cut only one of them in half."]], "trap": "A segment bisector alone guarantees equal halves, not a 90° angle.", "example": "A bisector creates halves 3x + 1 and x + 9. Set them equal: 3x + 1 = x + 9. Then x = 4 and each half is 13.", "desc": "A midpoint is the exact middle of a segment. The two little lengths are equal."}, "medium": {"parts": [["A midpoint makes equal halves", "If M is the midpoint of AB, AM = MB and AB = 2AM. A segment bisector is a line, ray, or segment passing through that midpoint."], ["Turn congruent halves into an equation", "A segment bisector passes through the midpoint, so the two halves are congruent. Set their length expressions equal, solve, and substitute to find each requested length."], ["Check what is actually bisected", "State which segment is bisected and identify its midpoint. Do not assume that both intersecting segments bisect each other without evidence."]], "trap": "A segment bisector alone guarantees equal halves, not a 90° angle.", "example": "A bisector creates halves 3x + 1 and x + 9. Set them equal: 3x + 1 = x + 9. Then x = 4 and each half is 13.", "desc": "If M is the midpoint of AB, AM = MB and AB = 2AM. A segment bisector is a line, ray, or segment passing through that midpoint."}, "expert": {"parts": [["A midpoint makes equal halves", "A midpoint partitions a segment into two congruent segments. A segment bisector need not be perpendicular; passing through the midpoint is the defining condition."], ["Turn congruent halves into an equation", "A bisector establishes a midpoint and equality of the two subsegment lengths. Use these facts with segment addition to relate a half-length to the whole. A general segment bisector need not be perpendicular."], ["Check what is actually bisected", "Incidence at a common point does not establish reciprocal bisection. Distinguish the bisecting object from the segment whose two parts are congruent."]], "trap": "A segment bisector alone guarantees equal halves, not a 90° angle.", "example": "A bisector creates halves 3x + 1 and x + 9. Set them equal: 3x + 1 = x + 9. Then x = 4 and each half is 13.", "desc": "A midpoint partitions a segment into two congruent segments. A segment bisector need not be perpendicular; passing through the midpoint is the defining condition."}}, "geo-slope": {"easy": {"parts": [["Slope measures steepness and direction", "Slope is the change in y divided by the change in x. A rise of 2 for a run of 1 gives slope 2."], ["Horizontal is zero; vertical is undefined", "A horizontal line has slope 0 because its vertical change is zero. A vertical line has undefined slope because division by zero is undefined."], ["Check whether points share a line", "Collinear points lie on the same line. Compare slopes using a shared point, or check whether all x-coordinates match for a vertical line."]], "trap": "Zero slope and undefined slope are different. A vertical line is x = a number, not y = a number.", "example": "Through (1,2) and (3,8), m = (8 − 2)/(3 − 1) = 6/2 = 3. Through two points with the same x-coordinate, slope is undefined.", "desc": "Slope is the change in y divided by the change in x. A rise of 2 for a run of 1 gives slope 2."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Slope measures steepness and direction", "m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁), when x₂ ≠ x₁. Keep endpoint order consistent. Positive slopes rise left to right; negative slopes fall."], ["Horizontal is zero; vertical is undefined", "A horizontal line has equation y = k and slope 0. A vertical line has equation x = k and undefined slope. Remember HOY / VUX."], ["Check whether points share a line", "For distinct points, equal slopes from a shared point show collinearity, provided the slopes exist. If all x-coordinates match, the points lie on one vertical line."]], "trap": "Zero slope and undefined slope are different. A vertical line is x = a number, not y = a number.", "example": "Through (1,2) and (3,8), m = (8 − 2)/(3 − 1) = 6/2 = 3. Through two points with the same x-coordinate, slope is undefined.", "desc": "m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁), when x₂ ≠ x₁. Keep endpoint order consistent. Positive slopes rise left to right; negative slopes fall."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Slope measures steepness and direction", "Slope is the ratio of vertical to horizontal coordinate change. Reversing both subtraction orders gives the same ratio; reversing only one changes the sign incorrectly."], ["Horizontal is zero; vertical is undefined", "For horizontal distinct points, Δy = 0 and Δx ≠ 0, giving m = 0. Vertical distinct points have Δx = 0, so their slope is undefined rather than infinite."], ["Check whether points share a line", "Collinear points lie on one line. Handle undefined slopes explicitly: common x-coordinates establish a vertical line, while equality of two defined slopes from a common point establishes nonvertical collinearity."]], "trap": "Zero slope and undefined slope are different. A vertical line is x = a number, not y = a number.", "example": "Through (1,2) and (3,8), m = (8 − 2)/(3 − 1) = 6/2 = 3. Through two points with the same x-coordinate, slope is undefined.", "desc": "Slope is the ratio of vertical to horizontal coordinate change. Reversing both subtraction orders gives the same ratio; reversing only one changes the sign incorrectly."}}, "geo-line-equations": {"easy": {"parts": [["Use y = mx + b", "In y = mx + b, m is the slope and b is the y-intercept. For m = 2 and b = 3, the equation is y = 2x + 3."], ["Use a point to find b", "For slope 3 through (2,7), substitute into y = 3x + b. Then 7 = 6 + b, so b = 1 and y = 3x + 1."], ["Check the finished rule", "Substitute the given point into your final equation. With two given points, check both. Handle a vertical line separately as x = a constant."]], "trap": "The b is a y-intercept, not whichever y-value appears in the given point.", "example": "Slope 2 through (3,7): 7 = 2(3) + b, so b = 1. The line is y = 2x + 1.", "desc": "In y = mx + b, m is the slope and b is the y-intercept. For m = 2 and b = 3, the equation is y = 2x + 3."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Use y = mx + b", "In y = mx + b, m is slope and (0,b) is the y-intercept. This form describes nonvertical lines. A vertical line is x = k."], ["Use a point to find b", "Find m, substitute a given point into y = mx + b, solve for b, then write the equation using variables x and y. With m = 3 through (2,7), y = 3x + 1."], ["Check the finished rule", "For y = 3x + 1, the point (2,7) works because 7 = 3(2) + 1. With two given points, check both; also check that the slope matches."]], "trap": "The b is a y-intercept, not whichever y-value appears in the given point.", "example": "Slope 2 through (3,7): 7 = 2(3) + b, so b = 1. The line is y = 2x + 1.", "desc": "In y = mx + b, m is slope and (0,b) is the y-intercept. This form describes nonvertical lines. A vertical line is x = k."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Use y = mx + b", "Slope-intercept form parameterizes nonvertical lines by slope and y-intercept. Horizontal lines fit with m = 0; vertical lines require a separate equation."], ["Use a point to find b", "Given two points, first compute their slope unless the x-coordinates match. Then solve b = y₁ − mx₁. Equivalent point-slope form is y − y₁ = m(x − x₁)."], ["Check the finished rule", "Verification checks both incidence and direction. A line through two distinct points is unique; a point and a specified slope likewise determine one line."]], "trap": "The b is a y-intercept, not whichever y-value appears in the given point.", "example": "Slope 2 through (3,7): 7 = 2(3) + b, so b = 1. The line is y = 2x + 1.", "desc": "Slope-intercept form parameterizes nonvertical lines by slope and y-intercept. Horizontal lines fit with m = 0; vertical lines require a separate equation."}}, "geo-angles": {"easy": {"parts": [["Name and measure an angle", "An angle is formed by two rays sharing a vertex. In ∠ABC, B is the vertex. Acute angles are below 90°, right angles equal 90°, obtuse angles lie between 90° and 180°, and straight angles equal 180°."], ["90, 180, or 360?", "Complementary angles total 90°; supplementary angles total 180°. A full turn is 360°. Opposite angles formed by intersecting lines are vertical angles and are equal."], ["Copy an angle with a compass", "Draw a new base ray. Use the same-radius arcs at the original and new vertices. Transfer the distance between the original arc marks onto the new arc, then draw the second ray."]], "trap": "A picture that looks like equal angles is not proof. Use markings, a stated bisector, or a known relationship.", "example": "Two adjacent angles measuring 28° and 47° form a 75° angle. The supplement of 75° is 180° − 75° = 105°.", "desc": "An angle is formed by two rays sharing a vertex. In ∠ABC, B is the vertex. Acute angles are below 90°, right angles equal 90°, obtuse angles lie between 90° and 180°, and straight angles equal 180°."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Name and measure an angle", "An angle is formed by two rays sharing a vertex. The vertex is the middle letter in a three-letter name. Acute: 0° < θ < 90°; right: 90°; obtuse: 90° < θ < 180°; straight: 180°."], ["90, 180, or 360?", "Complementary angles sum to 90°; supplementary angles sum to 180°. A linear pair forms a straight angle, so its angles sum to 180°. Angles around one point sum to 360°."], ["Copy an angle with a compass", "Draw a new base ray. From the original vertex, draw an arc crossing both angle rays. With the same radius, draw an arc from the new vertex. Transfer the distance between the original two arc intersections onto the new arc, then join the new vertex to that mark."]], "trap": "A picture that looks like equal angles is not proof. Use markings, a stated bisector, or a known relationship.", "example": "Two adjacent angles measuring 28° and 47° form a 75° angle. The supplement of 75° is 180° − 75° = 105°.", "desc": "An angle is formed by two rays sharing a vertex. The vertex is the middle letter in a three-letter name. Acute: 0° < θ < 90°; right: 90°; obtuse: 90° < θ < 180°; straight: 180°."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Name and measure an angle", "Angle names place the vertex between points on the two rays. A lone vertex letter is sufficient only when it unambiguously names the angle. Adjacent component measures add to the whole angle."], ["90, 180, or 360?", "Complementary and supplementary relationships concern sums, not necessarily adjacency. Vertical angles are congruent. Adjacent angles forming a straight line are supplementary."], ["Copy an angle with a compass", "Preserve the original vertex-to-arc radius in the new construction. Transfer the chord length between the original ray–arc intersections to the new arc. The resulting triangle has three matching side lengths, so the constructed angle is congruent to the original."]], "trap": "A picture that looks like equal angles is not proof. Use markings, a stated bisector, or a known relationship.", "example": "Two adjacent angles measuring 28° and 47° form a 75° angle. The supplement of 75° is 180° − 75° = 105°.", "desc": "Angle names place the vertex between points on the two rays. A lone vertex letter is sufficient only when it unambiguously names the angle. Adjacent component measures add to the whole angle."}}, "geo-angle-bisector": {"easy": {"parts": [["Bisect means two equal angles", "An angle bisector divides an angle into two congruent angles. Bisecting 110° gives two 55° angles. If the two halves are expressions, set them equal."], ["Incenter and incircle", "A triangle’s internal angle bisectors meet at the incenter. It is inside the triangle and equally distant from its sides. Its circle, the incircle, touches all three sides."]], "trap": "A picture that looks like equal angles is not proof. Use markings, a stated bisector, or a known relationship.", "example": "An angle bisector makes halves 3x + 5 and 5x − 15. Equate them: x = 10. Each half is 35°, so the whole angle is 70°.", "desc": "An angle bisector divides an angle into two congruent angles. Bisecting 110° gives two 55° angles. If the two halves are expressions, set them equal."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Bisect means two equal angles", "An angle bisector divides an angle into two congruent angles. For halves 3x + 5 and 5x − 15, equate them: x = 10, so each half is 35° and the whole is 70°."], ["Incenter and incircle", "The incenter is the common intersection of the triangle’s three internal angle bisectors. It is equidistant from the sides and is the center of the inscribed circle. Distance to a side is measured perpendicularly."]], "trap": "A picture that looks like equal angles is not proof. Use markings, a stated bisector, or a known relationship.", "example": "An angle bisector makes halves 3x + 5 and 5x − 15. Equate them: x = 10. Each half is 35°, so the whole angle is 70°.", "desc": "An angle bisector divides an angle into two congruent angles. For halves 3x + 5 and 5x − 15, equate them: x = 10, so each half is 35° and the whole is 70°."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Bisect means two equal angles", "A ray bisecting an angle creates equal angle measures, unlike an arbitrary interior ray. For a compass construction, mark equal distances on both rays, draw equal intersecting arcs from those marks, then connect the vertex to their intersection."], ["Incenter and incircle", "Two internal angle bisectors suffice to locate the incenter; the third passes through the same point. Use a perpendicular radius to a side to set the incircle radius, not the distance to a vertex."]], "trap": "A picture that looks like equal angles is not proof. Use markings, a stated bisector, or a known relationship.", "example": "An angle bisector makes halves 3x + 5 and 5x − 15. Equate them: x = 10. Each half is 35°, so the whole angle is 70°.", "desc": "A ray bisecting an angle creates equal angle measures, unlike an arbitrary interior ray. For a compass construction, mark equal distances on both rays, draw equal intersecting arcs from those marks, then connect the vertex to their intersection."}}, "geo-parallel": {"easy": {"parts": [["A transversal crosses two lines", "A transversal crosses two lines at different points. If those lines are parallel, corresponding and alternate angles are equal; same-side interior angles total 180°."], ["Spot matching or adding pairs", "Corresponding angles occupy matching positions. Alternate interior angles are between the lines and on opposite sides of the transversal."], ["Turn the relationship into an equation", "Choose the relationship before writing an equation. Equal angles 2x + 10 and 70 give 2x + 10 = 70. Same-side interior angles 2x and 3x give 2x + 3x = 180."]], "trap": "The special equal-angle rules require parallel lines. A transversal alone is not enough.", "example": "For parallel lines, same-side interior angles 2x and 3x satisfy 2x + 3x = 180. Thus x = 36 and the angles are 72° and 108°.", "desc": "A transversal crosses two lines at different points. If those lines are parallel, corresponding and alternate angles are equal; same-side interior angles total 180°."}, "medium": {"parts": [["A transversal crosses two lines", "For two distinct coplanar parallel lines cut by a transversal, corresponding, alternate interior, and alternate exterior angles are congruent. Same-side interior angles are supplementary."], ["Spot matching or adding pairs", "Corresponding angles occupy the same relative position at each intersection. Alternate interior pairs are between the lines on opposite sides of the transversal; alternate exterior pairs are outside on opposite sides."], ["Turn the relationship into an equation", "For parallel lines, corresponding angles 2x + 10 and 70 yield x = 30. Same-side interior angles 2x and 3x yield 5x = 180, so x = 36."]], "trap": "The special equal-angle rules require parallel lines. A transversal alone is not enough.", "example": "For parallel lines, same-side interior angles 2x and 3x satisfy 2x + 3x = 180. Thus x = 36 and the angles are 72° and 108°.", "desc": "For two distinct coplanar parallel lines cut by a transversal, corresponding, alternate interior, and alternate exterior angles are congruent. Same-side interior angles are supplementary."}, "expert": {"parts": [["A transversal crosses two lines", "Parallel lines in a plane do not intersect. The packet also treats coincident lines as having the same direction; here the usual distinct-line parallel-angle theorems use two separate parallel lines."], ["Spot matching or adding pairs", "Use position to identify the relationship before writing an equation. Parallelism establishes congruence for corresponding and alternate pairs; same-side interior measures sum to 180°."], ["Turn the relationship into an equation", "The relationship determines equality versus supplementation. Check the resulting angle measures against both the given expressions and the required total. Copying a corresponding angle through an external point constructs a parallel line."]], "trap": "The special equal-angle rules require parallel lines. A transversal alone is not enough.", "example": "For parallel lines, same-side interior angles 2x and 3x satisfy 2x + 3x = 180. Thus x = 36 and the angles are 72° and 108°.", "desc": "Parallel lines in a plane do not intersect. The packet also treats coincident lines as having the same direction; here the usual distinct-line parallel-angle theorems use two separate parallel lines."}}, "geo-perpendicular": {"easy": {"parts": [["Parallel lines share direction", "Parallel nonvertical lines have the same slope. Vertical lines are parallel to other distinct vertical lines."], ["Perpendicular lines make 90°", "Perpendicular lines meet at 90°. For a nonzero slope, use the negative reciprocal: 2/3 becomes −3/2. A horizontal line is perpendicular to a vertical line."], ["Find the new line through a point", "Choose the parallel or perpendicular slope first. Substitute the given point into y = mx + b to find b. For a vertical line, use x = a constant."]], "trap": "Changing only the sign is not enough: the perpendicular slope to 2 is −1/2, not −2.", "example": "A line perpendicular to y = 2x + 1 through (0,3) has slope −1/2, so its equation is y = −x/2 + 3.", "desc": "Parallel nonvertical lines have the same slope. Vertical lines are parallel to other distinct vertical lines."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Parallel lines share direction", "Distinct nonvertical parallel lines have equal slopes and different intercepts. Distinct vertical lines are parallel to each other."], ["Perpendicular lines make 90°", "Nonvertical perpendicular lines have slopes whose product is −1. Use the negative reciprocal. Horizontal and vertical lines are perpendicular to each other."], ["Find the new line through a point", "A line perpendicular to y = 2x + 1 through (0,3) has slope −1/2 and intercept 3: y = −x/2 + 3. For a vertical answer, use x = k."]], "trap": "Changing only the sign is not enough: the perpendicular slope to 2 is −1/2, not −2.", "example": "A line perpendicular to y = 2x + 1 through (0,3) has slope −1/2, so its equation is y = −x/2 + 3.", "desc": "Distinct nonvertical parallel lines have equal slopes and different intercepts. Distinct vertical lines are parallel to each other."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Parallel lines share direction", "Equal finite slopes give parallel or coincident lines. Distinct intercepts distinguish separate lines. Vertical parallel lines have undefined slopes, handled separately."], ["Perpendicular lines make 90°", "For nonzero finite m, a perpendicular slope is −1/m. The zero-slope case has a vertical perpendicular and cannot be handled by dividing by zero."], ["Find the new line through a point", "To construct a perpendicular through a point, identify two equal-distance intersections on the original line, then use equal arcs from those intersections to locate a second point on the perpendicular."]], "trap": "Changing only the sign is not enough: the perpendicular slope to 2 is −1/2, not −2.", "example": "A line perpendicular to y = 2x + 1 through (0,3) has slope −1/2, so its equation is y = −x/2 + 3.", "desc": "Equal finite slopes give parallel or coincident lines. Distinct intercepts distinguish separate lines. Vertical parallel lines have undefined slopes, handled separately."}}, "geo-centers": {"easy": {"parts": [["A perpendicular bisector does two jobs", "A perpendicular bisector passes through a segment’s midpoint at 90°. Every point on it is equally far from the two endpoints."], ["Write its equation", "Find the segment midpoint first. Use a slope perpendicular to the segment and make the new line pass through that midpoint."], ["Use equal endpoint distances", "P lies on the perpendicular bisector of AB. If PA = 2x + 3 and PB = x + 8, set them equal. Then x = 5 and both distances are 13."]], "trap": "Incenter uses angle bisectors and distances to sides. Circumcenter uses perpendicular bisectors and distances to vertices.", "example": "For the segment from (0,0) to (4,2), midpoint M = (2,1) and segment slope is 1/2. Its perpendicular bisector has equation y = −2x + 5.", "desc": "A perpendicular bisector passes through a segment’s midpoint at 90°. Every point on it is equally far from the two endpoints."}, "medium": {"parts": [["A perpendicular bisector does two jobs", "A perpendicular bisector passes through a segment’s midpoint at 90°. Every point on it is equidistant from the endpoints. To find its equation, combine the midpoint with a perpendicular slope."], ["Write its equation", "For A(0,0) and B(4,2), midpoint M = (2,1), segment slope = 1/2, and perpendicular slope = −2. Substituting M gives y = −2x + 5."], ["Use equal endpoint distances", "A point on the perpendicular bisector is equidistant from the endpoints. Use PA = PB to solve expressions such as 2x + 3 = x + 8."]], "trap": "Incenter uses angle bisectors and distances to sides. Circumcenter uses perpendicular bisectors and distances to vertices.", "example": "For the segment from (0,0) to (4,2), midpoint M = (2,1) and segment slope is 1/2. Its perpendicular bisector has equation y = −2x + 5.", "desc": "A perpendicular bisector passes through a segment’s midpoint at 90°. Every point on it is equidistant from the endpoints. To find its equation, combine the midpoint with a perpendicular slope."}, "expert": {"parts": [["A perpendicular bisector does two jobs", "The perpendicular bisector is the locus of points equidistant from the two endpoints. Coordinate construction uses the midpoint and negative reciprocal slope, with horizontal/vertical exceptions."], ["Write its equation", "Compute M = ((x₁+x₂)/2,(y₁+y₂)/2), then use the perpendicular direction through M. A horizontal segment has a vertical bisector; a vertical segment has a horizontal bisector."], ["Use equal endpoint distances", "Apply the perpendicular-bisector theorem to equate distances from a point on the bisector to the segment endpoints. Distinguish this equality from the equality of the two segment halves."]], "trap": "Incenter uses angle bisectors and distances to sides. Circumcenter uses perpendicular bisectors and distances to vertices.", "example": "For the segment from (0,0) to (4,2), midpoint M = (2,1) and segment slope is 1/2. Its perpendicular bisector has equation y = −2x + 5.", "desc": "The perpendicular bisector is the locus of points equidistant from the two endpoints. Coordinate construction uses the midpoint and negative reciprocal slope, with horizontal/vertical exceptions."}}, "geo-circumcenter": {"easy": {"parts": [["Circumcenter: equal distance to corners", "A triangle’s side perpendicular bisectors meet at the circumcenter. It is equally far from all three vertices. A circle centered there passes through the vertices, and the center can be outside the triangle."], ["Find it with two perpendicular bisectors", "Construct the perpendicular bisectors of two sides. Their intersection is the circumcenter. Keep the compass centered there and open it to any vertex to draw the circle."]], "trap": "Incenter uses angle bisectors and distances to sides. Circumcenter uses perpendicular bisectors and distances to vertices.", "example": "A right triangle with vertices (0,0), (6,0), and (0,8) has circumcenter (3,4), the midpoint of its hypotenuse. Each vertex is 5 units from that center.", "desc": "A triangle’s side perpendicular bisectors meet at the circumcenter. It is equally far from all three vertices. A circle centered there passes through the vertices, and the center can be outside the triangle."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Circumcenter: equal distance to corners", "The circumcenter is the intersection of the side perpendicular bisectors. It is equidistant from the three vertices and is the center of the circumscribed circle. It can lie outside the triangle."], ["Find it with two perpendicular bisectors", "Two side perpendicular bisectors locate the circumcenter. Use the distance from that point to any vertex as the radius of the circumcircle. All three vertices must lie on the circle."]], "trap": "Incenter uses angle bisectors and distances to sides. Circumcenter uses perpendicular bisectors and distances to vertices.", "example": "A right triangle with vertices (0,0), (6,0), and (0,8) has circumcenter (3,4), the midpoint of its hypotenuse. Each vertex is 5 units from that center.", "desc": "The circumcenter is the intersection of the side perpendicular bisectors. It is equidistant from the three vertices and is the center of the circumscribed circle. It can lie outside the triangle."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Circumcenter: equal distance to corners", "The circumcenter is inside an acute triangle, at the hypotenuse midpoint of a right triangle, and outside an obtuse triangle. Construct two side perpendicular bisectors, then use the distance to any vertex as the circumradius."], ["Find it with two perpendicular bisectors", "Perpendicular-bisector intersections are equidistant from the triangle vertices. Two bisectors suffice; the third is concurrent. For coordinate problems, solve the equations of two perpendicular bisectors."]], "trap": "Incenter uses angle bisectors and distances to sides. Circumcenter uses perpendicular bisectors and distances to vertices.", "example": "A right triangle with vertices (0,0), (6,0), and (0,8) has circumcenter (3,4), the midpoint of its hypotenuse. Each vertex is 5 units from that center.", "desc": "The circumcenter is inside an acute triangle, at the hypotenuse midpoint of a right triangle, and outside an obtuse triangle. Construct two side perpendicular bisectors, then use the distance to any vertex as the circumradius."}}, "geo-angle-strategy": {"easy": {"parts": [["Start with one certain relationship", "Start with a known relationship: a straight angle is 180°, a triangle totals 180°, and a full turn is 360°. Use markings and stated facts, rather than how the diagram looks."], ["Name the unknown and solve", "Write an equation using the angle relationship. For triangle angles x, 2x, and 3x, solve 6x = 180. Then x = 30°, so the angles are 30°, 60°, and 90°."], ["Add a helpful line when it unlocks a clue", "An auxiliary line is an extra line added for a specific reason. For example, drawing a parallel through a vertex can reveal equal alternate interior angles. Explain why the added line helps."]], "trap": "If the problem asks for an angle written 3x + 5, finding x is only the first step. Substitute to finish.", "example": "Triangle angles x + 10, 2x, and 80° give 3x + 90 = 180. Then x = 30°, and the three angles are 40°, 60°, and 80°.", "desc": "Start with a known relationship: a straight angle is 180°, a triangle totals 180°, and a full turn is 360°. Use markings and stated facts, rather than how the diagram looks."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Start with one certain relationship", "Identify the smallest useful relationship: linear pair, vertical angles, triangle sum, full turn, bisector, or parallel-line angle pair. Mark known values before solving."], ["Name the unknown and solve", "For triangle angles x, 2x, and 3x, write x + 2x + 3x = 180. Then x = 30°, giving angles 30°, 60°, and 90°."], ["Add a helpful line when it unlocks a clue", "An auxiliary line is a line you add to a diagram to make useful relationships visible. A parallel through a vertex can reveal alternate interior or corresponding angles. Explain the reason for adding it."]], "trap": "If the problem asks for an angle written 3x + 5, finding x is only the first step. Substitute to finish.", "example": "Triangle angles x + 10, 2x, and 80° give 3x + 90 = 180. Then x = 30°, and the three angles are 40°, 60°, and 80°.", "desc": "Identify the smallest useful relationship: linear pair, vertical angles, triangle sum, full turn, bisector, or parallel-line angle pair. Mark known values before solving."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Start with one certain relationship", "Use the givens and established theorems to justify each equation. Diagram appearance alone does not establish parallelism, perpendicularity, or congruence."], ["Name the unknown and solve", "Translate the angle relationship into an algebraic equation, solve it, then evaluate every requested angle expression. An algebraic variable is not always itself the angle measure."], ["Add a helpful line when it unlocks a clue", "An auxiliary construction must have a specified property, such as passing through a point parallel to a given line. Use that property to justify subsequent equalities, then check all angle sums and constraints."]], "trap": "If the problem asks for an angle written 3x + 5, finding x is only the first step. Substitute to finish.", "example": "Triangle angles x + 10, 2x, and 80° give 3x + 90 = 180. Then x = 30°, and the three angles are 40°, 60°, and 80°.", "desc": "Use the givens and established theorems to justify each equation. Diagram appearance alone does not establish parallelism, perpendicularity, or congruence."}}}};
TOPICS.push(...GEOMETRY.topics);QUESTIONS.push(...GEOMETRY.questions);
Object.assign(READING_CONTENT.topics,GEOMETRY.reading);
